We present a field-theoretic formulation of gravitation where gravity is treated not as a deformation of spacetime geometry, but as a symmetric tensor field $h_{\mu\nu}$ propagating on a flat, non-dynamical Minkowski background $\eta_{\mu\nu}$. In this approach, the Riemannian metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ arises as an effective composite field governing the dynamics of matter. We investigate the internal structure of black holes within this framework, demonstrating that the effective metric components remain smooth, finite, and regular throughout the interior region, thereby avoiding the non-physical coordinate and physical singularities of standard General Relativity. Furthermore, we analyze the vacuum state of quantum fields within this Minkowski-based field representation. We demonstrate that the self-consistent coupling between the field and the background leads to an exact cancellation of the quantum vacuum energy density, causing the zero-point energy contribution to the gravitational equations to vanish identically. This offers a natural resolution to the cosmological constant problem while preserving compatibility with macroscopic gravitational phenomena.
